Actually, let me reconsider the fast loop once more. The fast loop:
```c
count = (int32_t)((pDestLimit - pDest) / 3);
srcLength = (int32_t)(pSrcLimit - pSrc);
if(count > srcLength) {
    count = srcLength;
}
if(count < 3) {
    break;
}
do {
    ch=*pSrc++;
    if(ch <= 0x7f) {
        *pDest++ = (uint8_t)ch;   // 1 byte
    } ...
} while(--count > 0);
```

For ASCII, each iteration writes 1 byte. `count = min((pDestLimit-pDest)/3, srcLength)`. So the fast loop writes at most `count` bytes. Since `count <= (pDestLimit-pDest)/3`, the fast loop writes at most `(pDestLimit-pDest)/3` bytes. Then the slow loop handles the rest. No overflow.

So `u_strToUTF8` is safe for ASCII input. 

